Phoenix RPi4: chain glamor's DestroyPixmap screen hook instead of short-cutting
straight to fbDestroyPixmap.

glamor_init() saves the previous screen->DestroyPixmap into
glamor_priv->saved_procs.destroy_pixmap and installs glamor_destroy_pixmap(),
but that function ends in a hard `return fbDestroyPixmap(pixmap);` -- it never
calls the saved pointer, which is only ever read again to restore the hook at
CloseScreen. So anything wrapped BELOW glamor is cut out of the chain.

Upstream xf86 never notices: glamor_init() runs at ScreenInit and DamageSetup()
runs later at extension-init time, so damage always wraps ABOVE glamor. This
DDX brings damage up through shadowSetup() (tools/x11-port/ddx/fbdev.c:591)
BEFORE glamor_init() (:603), so damage sits below glamor -- in exactly the slot
glamor does not honour.

Consequence: damageDestroyPixmap() never ran for ANY pixmap, so damage.c's
teardown loop (damage.c:1493-1501) never destroyed DamagePtrs attached to dying
pixmaps. glamor's own stipple damage then outlived its drawable, and at desktop
exit FreeGC() (dix/gc.c:781) released gc->stipple before calling the DDX
DestroyGC (:783) -> glamor_destroy_gc -> glamor_invalidate_stipple ->
DamageUnregister(), which dereferenced the freed pixmap:

  pc=0x51d444 (dixGetPrivate+0x68, `ldr x0,[x0]`), far=0x60, x1=0
  => pScreen->devPrivates read from a recycled chunk

Measured on hardware: pDamage/pDrawable at the fault were exactly the pair
glamor had registered, and an instrumented damageDestroyPixmap was never
entered for that pixmap.

Fix: add a screen-hook-only wrapper that does the same FBO teardown and then
calls saved_procs.destroy_pixmap. glamor_destroy_pixmap() itself is _X_EXPORT
and is called directly from ~15 places inside glamor to drop private pixmaps --
those must NOT traverse the screen chain -- so the exported function is left
untouched and only the hook slot changes. The wrapper reinstates its own slot
after the downstream call because damageDestroyPixmap() unwraps and re-wraps
screen->DestroyPixmap with itself.
--- a/glamor/glamor.c
+++ b/glamor/glamor.c
@@ -272,6 +272,47 @@
     return fbDestroyPixmap(pixmap);
 }
 
+/*
+ * Screen-hook form of the above: tears down the FBO exactly as
+ * glamor_destroy_pixmap() does, but then chains to whatever was installed below
+ * glamor instead of jumping straight to fbDestroyPixmap().
+ *
+ * glamor_destroy_pixmap() is _X_EXPORT and is called directly from ~15 places
+ * inside glamor to drop a private pixmap; those callers must NOT go through the
+ * screen chain, so the exported function is left alone and only the hook slot
+ * changes.
+ *
+ * Why this matters here: upstream xf86 runs glamor_init() at ScreenInit and
+ * DamageSetup() later at extension-init time, so damage always wraps ABOVE
+ * glamor and glamor's short-cut to fbDestroyPixmap() is harmless. This DDX
+ * brings damage up via shadowSetup() BEFORE glamor_init(), so damage sits below
+ * glamor -- in the one slot glamor did not honour. The result was that
+ * damageDestroyPixmap() never ran for any pixmap, so damage.c's teardown loop
+ * never destroyed DamagePtrs attached to dying pixmaps. glamor's own stipple
+ * damage then outlived its drawable and DamageUnregister() faulted on freed
+ * memory at desktop exit.
+ */
+static Bool
+glamor_destroy_pixmap_hook(PixmapPtr pixmap)
+{
+    ScreenPtr screen = pixmap->drawable.pScreen;
+    glamor_screen_private *glamor_priv = glamor_get_screen_private(screen);
+    Bool ret;
+
+    if (pixmap->refcnt == 1) {
+        glamor_pixmap_destroy_fbo(pixmap);
+    }
+
+    ret = (*glamor_priv->saved_procs.destroy_pixmap) (pixmap);
+
+    /* The callee below us may be a wrapper that unwraps and re-wraps
+     * screen->DestroyPixmap with ITSELF (damageDestroyPixmap does exactly
+     * that), which would drop us out of the chain. Reinstate our slot. */
+    screen->DestroyPixmap = glamor_destroy_pixmap_hook;
+
+    return ret;
+}
+
 void
 glamor_block_handler(ScreenPtr screen)
 {
@@ -662,7 +703,7 @@
     screen->CloseScreen = glamor_close_screen;
 
     glamor_priv->saved_procs.destroy_pixmap = screen->DestroyPixmap;
-    screen->DestroyPixmap = glamor_destroy_pixmap;
+    screen->DestroyPixmap = glamor_destroy_pixmap_hook;
 
     /* If we are using egl screen, call egl screen init to
      * register correct close screen function. */
